    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1 - Prioritize outfit cosmetics:</strong> Outfit cosmetics are visible to yourself and other players, maximizing cosmetic enjoyment. Ship cosmetics are primarily for other players' appreciation. Allocate cosmetic budgets toward outfit cosmetics for personal satisfaction.</p><p><strong>Tip 2 - Use seasonal cosmetics for variety:</strong> Sea of Thieves provides cosmetics through seasonal progression. Combine purchased cosmetics with earned cosmetics to maximize cosmetic variety within cosmetic budget constraints.</p><p><strong>Tip 3 - Avoid cosmetic bundles:</strong> Limited-time cosmetic bundles occasionally offer discounts but often include cosmetics with limited individual appeal. Evaluate individual cosmetics before purchasing bundles.</p><p><strong>Tip 4 - Evaluate pirate fantasy appeal:</strong> Sea of Thieves cosmetics emphasize pirate fantasy themes. Avoid purchasing cosmetics from themes with limited personal connection, as they may feel less desirable upon acquisition.</p><p><strong>Tip 5 - Plan cosmetic purchases around seasonal events:</strong> Sea of Thieves seasonal events introduce thematic cosmetics. Plan cosmetic spending to coordinate with seasonal event engagement for maximum cosmetic relevance.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>Sea of Thieves' cosmetic monetization represents a strong ethical standard for live-service multiplayer games. Rare maintains cosmetic pricing at moderate levels, provides alternative cosmetic acquisition through seasonal progression, and avoids gacha mechanics despite competitive cosmetic monetization pressure. The cosmetic ecosystem successfully monetizes appearance customization while preserving player agency and cosmetic accessibility.</p><p>The pirate fantasy theme provides inherent cosmetic appeal that justifies cosmetic investment without relying on predatory mechanics. Players seeking pirate self-expression find cosmetics genuinely useful for roleplay and fantasy immersion. This thematic appeal allows sustainable cosmetic monetization without manipulative pricing or artificial scarcity.</p><p>For players valuing ethical cosmetics in multiplayer games, Sea of Thieves represents a model of responsible monetization. The cosmetic system respects player fairness while maintaining financial sustainability. The measured cosmetic release schedule and transparent pricing prevent cosmetic monetization from dominating the player experience. Sea of Thieves demonstrates that cosmetic fairness and profitability align strategically when developers prioritize long-term player value over short-term revenue extraction.</p>`,
