    editorial_html: `<p>World of Warcraft represents the maximum cosmetic monetization extraction possible while maintaining ethical compliance in developed markets. Blizzard's strategy of layering cosmetics atop mandatory subscription creates compound monetization that significantly exceeds cosmetic-only games. A player paying $15/month subscription plus $20-30/month cosmetics spending totals $35-45/month for single game, representing extreme per-game spending relative to industry standards.</p><p>The subscription-plus-cosmetics model legitimizes cosmetic pricing that would be immediately rejected if cosmetic-only. Players accepting $15/month subscription overhead develop spending mindset that higher cosmetics pricing feels acceptable within larger expenditure context. This psychological priming effect increases willingness to spend on cosmetics relative to cosmetic-only games at identical prices.</p><p>World of Warcraft's cosmetic escalation over two decades demonstrates how gradually escalating monetization can shift baseline player expectations. Cosmetics that were entirely gameplay-earned in 2004 became cosmetic-shop-only by 2024. This gradual transition obscured aggregate monetization changes while establishing new normal cosmetic pricing expectations. For players considering World of Warcraft engagement, comprehensive budgeting for both subscription and cosmetics is essential to understand true financial commitment. The cosmetic system represents monetization sophistication that maximizes revenue through compound pricing tracks.</p>`,
