    editorial_html: `<p>Stumble Guys exemplifies how free-to-play cosmetic monetization can prey on younger players through design patterns explicitly identified as predatory. The combination of gacha mechanics, opaque pricing, FOMO-driven cosmetics, and youth targeting creates systematic exploitation designed to maximize spending from players with developing financial judgment. Parents allowing children unsupervised cosmetic access in Stumble Guys should expect excessive spending and psychological manipulation.</p><p>Scopely's business model demonstrates that predatory cosmetic design remains viable in mobile gaming despite industry awareness of ethical concerns. The company clearly prioritizes cosmetic revenue extraction over player wellbeing, employing sophisticated psychological pressure tactics designed to exploit youth. The lack of transparent pricing or pity mechanics suggests intentional opacity designed to prevent informed spending decisions.</p><p>Stumble Guys should be actively avoided by players concerned with ethical cosmetic monetization. The game's predatory design patterns, youth targeting, and lack of transparency distinguish it as among the most exploitative cosmetic systems currently operating. Parents should strongly consider preventing children from accessing Stumble Guys' cosmetic system entirely. For players seeking party games with ethical cosmetics, Fall Guys represents a substantially fairer alternative despite imperfect monetization.</p>`,
